redentials out of source files and command history. Validate rendered manifests before they reach a production cluster.\n\nFor Helm-based deployments, review the chart package, values, rendered resources, and release history. For Argo CD-based delivery, review the source revision, destination, Application configuration, project policy, synchronization settings, and resulting resource state.\n\n### 6. Verification and operational evidence\n\nVerify the Application source revision, destination cluster and namespace, synchronization status, health status, and resource tree. Inspect the generated manifests and compare them with live resources. Confirm that the destination workload is healthy through Kubernetes conditions, Pod readiness, Service connectivity, and application-level checks. A Synced status alone does not prove application availability.\n\nUseful evidence includes the source commit or chart version, rendered manifests, deployment command or synchronization record, Kubernetes events, controller status, Pod conditions, application logs, and relevant monitoring signals. Capture evidence before changing a failing release so the original failure can be investigated accurately.\n\n### 7. Troubleshooting approach\n\nIf the operation fails, isolate the failure layer before applying a correction.\n\n- Check whether the source repository or chart package can be accessed.\n- Validate chart metadata, dependencies, values, templates, or manifest generation.\n- Confirm that the correct cluster, namespace, and target revision are selected.\n- Check credentials and authorization separately.\n- Inspect Kubernetes API responses and resource events.\n- Check whether controllers created the expected resources.\n- Inspect Pod scheduling, image retrieval, startup, probes, and application logs.\n- Confirm the resulting state after remediation.\n\nAvoid deleting releases, pruning resources, or manually editing production objects as an initial troubleshooting step. Understand ownership and data impact first.\n\n### 8. CI/CD and GitOps integration\n\nA mature pipeline should validate source changes before deployment. For Helm, this includes chart linting, dependency validation, template rendering, manifest review, and controlled release promotion. For Argo CD, it includes pull-request review, repository validation, Application policy checks, controlled synchronization, and post-deployment health verification.\n\nKeep application image versions separate from chart versions. Promote the same tested image digest through environments. Record the source commit, chart version, configuration revision, target environment, and deployment result so that every release can be traced and audited.\n\n### 9. Security and production practices\n\nProtect Argo CD using SSO or another approved authentication mechanism, narrowly scoped RBAC policies, repository credentials stored through supported secure mechanisms, and controlled destination-cluster permissions. Avoid broad administrator access for routine application operations. Review who can modify repositories, Application resources, project policies, and synchronization settings. Do not expose repository tokens or private keys in logs, Git files, or terminal output.\n\nUse separate permissions for development and production, protect deployment credentials, review changes to synchronization and pruning settings, and restrict who can modify deployment sources. Monitor release failures, application health, synchronization delays, and unexpected live-state changes.\n\n### 10. Practical engineer checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct repository, chart or application, and version.\n- Review configuration and environment-specific values.\n- Validate templates or generated manifests.\n- Confirm destination cluster and namespace.\n- Check credentials and least-privilege permissions.\n- Review the expected resource changes before applying them.\n- Monitor the release or synchronization operation.\n- Verify Kubernetes workload and application health.\n- Record the source revision and deployment evidence.\n- Document the root cause and recovery steps for failures."
